etd:discourse_theme.description%7C15 a etd:DiscourseUnit ;
    rdfs:label "DiscourseUnit: theme.description"@en ;
    etd:chunk_id "15:chunk:0"^^xsd:string ;
    etd:chunk_index 0 ;
    etd:chunk_index_unknown false ;
    etd:chunk_total 1 ;
    etd:contextText """**Domain**: Insurance/Risk Assessment  \r
**Description**: AI-powered insurance underwriting systems show bias in risk assessment and pricing, particularly affecting minority communities and certain geographic areas.\r
\r
**Ethical Challenges**:\r
\r
* **Proxy Discrimination**: Use of zip codes and other proxies that correlate with protected characteristics  \r
* **Historical Data Bias**: Training on historical data that reflects past discriminatory practices  \r
* **Disparate Impact**: Higher premiums for equivalent risk profiles in minority communities  \r
* **Regulatory Compliance**: Violation of fair lending and anti-discrimination laws\r
\r
**Public Datasets**:\r
\r
* **Communities and Crime Dataset**: UCI repository with socioeconomic and crime data  \r
* **URL**: https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/183/communities+and+crime  \r
* **German Credit Dataset**: Classic fairness benchmark for credit risk assessment  \r
* **URL**: https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/144/statlog+german+credit+data  \r
* **Default of Credit Card Clients**: Taiwan dataset for credit default prediction  \r
* **URL**: https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/350/default+of+credit+card+clients"""^^xsd:string ;
    etd:context_source "dataset_theme"^^xsd:string ;
    etd:datasetId "ai_ethics_datasets"^^xsd:string ;
    etd:discourse_id 15 ;
    etd:extractedFrom etd:discourse_theme.description%7C15 ;
    etd:field "theme.description"^^xsd:string ;
    etd:hasSourceEntity <https://alfie-project.eu/etd-hub/entity/person/expert-4-c84647f9>,
        <https://alfie-project.eu/etd-hub/entity/person/expert-5-36a8e629>,
        <https://alfie-project.eu/etd-hub/entity/theme/theme-15-f6e4b65b> ;
    etd:runId "run-b0eafdda0235eb48"^^xsd:string ;
    etd:source "structural"^^xsd:string ;
    etd:source_doc_id "15"^^xsd:string .

etd:discourse_answer.description%7C11 a etd:DiscourseUnit ;
    rdfs:label "DiscourseUnit: answer.description"@en ;
    etd:chunk_id "11:chunk:0"^^xsd:string ;
    etd:chunk_index 0 ;
    etd:chunk_index_unknown false ;
    etd:chunk_total 1 ;
    etd:contextText """A predictive system like COMPAS cannot truly be considered fair if it lacks transparency and exhibits systemic racial disparities; even if trained on “real-world” data. Fairness in algorithmic decision-making, especially in high-stakes domains like criminal justice, goes beyond technical accuracy; it requires accountability, interpretability, and equity in outcomes.\r
\r
ProPublica’s 2016 investigation into COMPAS found that Black defendants were twice as likely as white defendants to be falsely labeled high-risk. Northpointe (now Equivant) contested this, arguing that the system maintained overall accuracy parity. This disagreement highlights a critical issue in the field: fairness criteria often conflict. As noted by Kleinberg et al. (2016), you cannot simultaneously satisfy predictive parity, equal false positive rates, and calibration across groups when base rates differ, as they often do in racially stratified societies.\r
\r
Moreover, the use of proprietary, black-box algorithms in sentencing decisions raises serious concerns about due process. Defendants, lawyers, and judges cannot meaningfully challenge or understand a system that lacks algorithmic transparency. This undermines legal principles such as the right to contest evidence and equal protection under the law.\r
\r
Training on “real-world” data is no guarantee of fairness either. Historical data from the criminal justice system reflects decades of systemic bias, from over-policing in Black communities to harsher sentencing for similar offenses. Algorithms trained on this data risk codifying and perpetuating these inequities (Barocas & Selbst, 2016).\r
\r
References: \r
ProPublica (2016). Machine Bias – https://www.propublica.org/article/machine-bias-risk-assessments-in-criminal-sentencing\r
\r
Kleinberg, Mullainathan, & Raghavan (2016). Inherent Trade-Offs in the Fair Determination of Risk Scores\r
\r
Barocas & Selbst (2016). Big Data's Disparate Impact, California Law Review"""^^xsd:string ;
    etd:context_source "dataset_answer"^^xsd:string ;
    etd:datasetId "ai_ethics_datasets"^^xsd:string ;
    etd:discourse_id 11 ;
    etd:extractedFrom etd:discourse_answer.description%7C11 ;
