etd:discourse_theme.description%7C13 a etd:DiscourseUnit ;
    rdfs:label "DiscourseUnit: theme.description"@en ;
    etd:chunk_id "13:chunk:0"^^xsd:string ;
    etd:chunk_index 0 ;
    etd:chunk_index_unknown false ;
    etd:chunk_total 1 ;
    etd:contextText """**Domain**: Transportation/Gig Economy  \r
**Description**: Pandey & Caliskan (2021) analyzed 100+ million rideshare samples in Chicago, finding that dynamic pricing algorithms systematically discriminate against neighborhoods with non-white and low-income populations.\r
\r
**Ethical Challenges**:\r
\r
* **Geographic Bias**: Neighborhoods with larger non-white populations charged significantly higher fares  \r
* **Socioeconomic Bias**: Higher poverty areas face pricing discrimination  \r
* **Algorithmic Opacity**: Dynamic pricing systems without transparency  \r
* **Historical Discrimination Reinforcement**: Perpetuation of historical transportation discrimination\r
\r
**Public Datasets**:\r
\r
* **Primary**: Chicago Transportation Network Provider (TNP) Trips Dataset  \r
* **URL**: https://data.cityofchicago.org/Transportation/Transportation-Network-Providers-Trips/m6dm-c72p  \r
* **Source**: City of Chicago Open Data Portal  \r
* **GitHub Analysis**: https://github.com/toddwschneider/chicago-taxi-data  \r
* **Dashboard**: https://toddwschneider.com/dashboards/chicago-taxi-ridehailing-data/  \r
* **Content**: 100+ million rideshare samples analyzed (2018-present)  \r
* **Includes**: Uber, Lyft, Via data with anonymized fare, time, and census tract location information"""^^xsd:string ;
    etd:context_source "dataset_theme"^^xsd:string ;
    etd:datasetId "ai_ethics_datasets"^^xsd:string ;
    etd:discourse_id 13 ;
    etd:extractedFrom etd:discourse_theme.description%7C13 ;
    etd:field "theme.description"^^xsd:string ;
    etd:hasSourceEntity <https://alfie-project.eu/etd-hub/entity/person/expert-4-c84647f9>,
        <https://alfie-project.eu/etd-hub/entity/person/expert-5-36a8e629>,
        <https://alfie-project.eu/etd-hub/entity/theme/theme-13-714c9911> ;
    etd:runId "run-b0eafdda0235eb48"^^xsd:string ;
    etd:source "structural"^^xsd:string ;
    etd:source_doc_id "13"^^xsd:string .

etd:discourse_theme.description%7C12 a etd:DiscourseUnit ;
    rdfs:label "DiscourseUnit: theme.description"@en ;
    etd:chunk_id "12:chunk:0"^^xsd:string ;
    etd:chunk_index 0 ;
    etd:chunk_index_unknown false ;
    etd:chunk_total 1 ;
    etd:contextText """**Domain**: Computer Vision/Biometrics  \r
**Description**: Joy Buolamwini (MIT) and Timnit Gebru's Gender Shades project exposed severe intersectional bias in commercial facial recognition systems, demonstrating these systems performed 32 times worse on dark-skinned women compared to light-skinned men.\r
\r
**Ethical Challenges**:\r
\r
* **Intersectional Bias**: Error rates varied dramatically: 0.8% for light-skinned men vs. up to 34.7% for dark-skinned women  \r
* **Data Representation**: Training datasets with 83.5% white individuals  \r
* **Civil Rights Impact**: Use in surveillance and law enforcement applications  \r
* **Platform Governance**: Led to policy changes at major tech companies\r
\r
**Public Datasets**:\r
\r
* **Primary**: Pilot Parliaments Benchmark (PPB)  \r
* **Source**: Algorithmic Justice League  \r
* **URL**: Contact datasets@ajlunited.org for access  \r
* **MIT Media Lab**: https://www.media.mit.edu/projects/gender-shades/faq/  \r
* **Research Paper**: https://www.media.mit.edu/publications/gender-shades-intersectional-accuracy-disparities-in-commercial-gender-classification/  \r
* **Content**: 1,270 unique individuals from 6 countries (3 African, 3 European)  \r
* **Features**: Balanced representation, 44.39% women, 47% dark-skin representation  \r
* **Labels**: Fitzpatrick Skin Type classification and gender annotations"""^^xsd:string ;
    etd:context_source "dataset_theme"^^xsd:string ;
    etd:datasetId "ai_ethics_datasets"^^xsd:string ;
    etd:discourse_id 12 ;
    etd:extractedFrom etd:discourse_theme.description%7C12 ;
